  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Warren delivered six innings of one-run baseball while the offense broke through early.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what control looks like from the top. Warren allowed four hits over six frames with eight strikeouts, and the Yankees never trailed. They took the lead in the second inning and built it to five runs by the third, when Garc\u00eda Jr. hit a two-run shot that made it 4-0. Colorado got one back immediately, but the game was already decided. The bullpen closed it without drama. Schreiber, Blackburn, and Fulmer each threw a clean inning. This wasn't a desperate escape or a late rally. This was a team that got pitching, got runs early, and held on without requiring anything else.",
    "what_to_watch": "What matters is the clean early separation. The offense converted just one of five RISP situations, yet it came in the inning that mattered most. The lineup left runners on base three times and still scored six runs. That's efficiency over volume. Warren's six innings of one-run baseball gave them permission to play ahead the whole way. No late-inning pressure, no recovery required. When the starting pitcher eats innings and you score four in one inning, the math works. Two wins straight now, and this one felt uncomplicated in a way that builds confidence instead of consuming it.",
